Here is the commit message:
Call AbstractElement getID() insteado of getId()

Relying on capitalization significance to differentiate methods on
this object seems like asking for trouble.  For now, just fix the
capitalization bug, but we might want to
refactor getID() and getId() to more distinct names.

Here is the root cause:
The `equals()` method in `AbstractElement` mistakenly called the generic Blueprints-style method `getId()` (which returns a boxed `Object` ID, potentially inconsistent, not guaranteed unique/comparable, or referring to a different representation of identity) instead of the Titan-internal method `getID()` (which returns the primitive `long` ID used for genuine element identity within the graph). Because `getId()` and `getID()` differ only in capitalization, the developer accidentally relied on the wrong accessor, causing the equality check to be based on an incorrect or inconsistent identity value rather than the true internal vertex/edge ID. This subtle naming collision made the bug easy to introduce and hard to detect through code review alone.

Here is the patch:

     @Override
     public boolean equals(Object other) {
         if (this==other) {
             return true;
         } else if (other==null) {
             return false;
         } else if (this instanceof TitanVertex && other instanceof TitanVertex) {
-            return getId().equals(((TitanVertex)other).getId());
+            return getID() == ((TitanVertex)other).getID();
         } else if (this instanceof TitanEdge && other instanceof TitanEdge) {
-            return getId().equals(((TitanEdge)other).getId());
+            return getID() == ((TitanEdge)other).getID();
         } else {
             return false;
         }
     }



